{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792338111,"nonce":"LkDaPwhzcF/HauyMj2wEQC53lx8=","title":"A wasm side module built with a Rust 1.101 nightly cannot be loaded: its std imports pthread_key_*","message":"**What happens**\n`ash --build` refuses a native library beside the output when it imports functions ash_std does not define. A side module built with a Rust nightly from October 2026 (1.101.0-nightly, first seen with 2026-10-12, not with the 2026-08-25 one of 1.100.0) imports pthread_key_create, pthread_key_delete and pthread_setspecific, three more than before (27 function imports against 24), and Ash reports: \"a native library beside the output imports 3 function(s) this runtime does not define, the first few being pthread_key_create, pthread_key_delete, pthread_setspecific.\" Ash main at 2026-10-18 does it too.\n\n**Seen in**\nhlwgpu's `gpu` workflow, whose browser job builds xgpu.wasm with the latest nightly and runs it on Ash's published `nightly` (which was last published 2026-08-25); every side module of the web stack is affected the same way: xgpu.wasm, xwindow.wasm and blinc_abi.wasm. Rust's thread-local destructors on wasm32-wasip1-threads moved to pthread keys.\n\n**Worked around**\nThe workflows that build web files pin `nightly-2026-08-25`, and the three build_wasm.sh scripts stop with a message when a build imports pthread_key_create, naming RUST_NIGHTLY.\n\n**Wanted**\nash_std defining pthread_key_create, pthread_key_delete and pthread_setspecific (per-thread, as a side module's thread-local destructors need), so a current nightly's modules load, and a new published nightly of Ash: its release has not been rebuilt since 2026-08-25.","files":null}]}